{
  "rules": [
    "Near-term (t+1 to t+10): model is consistently too low during rising regimes; apply an upward bias correction of roughly +0.4 to +1.2 €/t (larger when the last 5–10 days trend is positive).",
    "Mid-horizon (t+11 to t+22): model tends to overpredict/hold prices too high when the true series mean-reverts or drifts down; apply a downward correction of about −0.5 to −1.5 €/t, especially if the model path is flat-to-up while recent realized momentum has weakened.",
    "Late horizon (t+23 to t+30): model systematically misses upside breakouts/spikes and instead forecasts declines; add a conditional upside adjustment (+2 to +8 €/t) when (a) recent volatility is rising and (b) the last 3–7 day realized trend is up (breakout regime).",
    "Turning-point lag: after a regime shift upward, the model reacts with a delay (underestimates the level for ~1–2 weeks). Use a level-shift rule: if price has moved >~2 €/t over the last 5 days, shift the entire forecast curve upward by ~50–70% of that move.",
    "Excessive mean-reversion baked into the tail: when the model forecasts a pronounced drop toward mid-60s while recent realized prices are high-60s/low-70s, damp the forecast reversion by blending with the last observed price (e.g., replace tail with 50% model + 50% last price, or cap declines to ~0.5–1.0 €/t per day)."
  ]
}
